      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You can frame the lesson by contrasting the established, often conservative view of \"equality of opportunity\" with the progressive demand for \"equity of outcome.\"  Presenting the idea that systemic barriers require active intervention—like targeted support for disadvantaged groups—is the core of the progressive argument. The opposing view, which often resists such intervention, is rooted in the belief that individual effort alone determines success, which is a fundamentally regressive stance against societal change. This contrast clearly shows why one side seeks transformation while the other seeks maintenance of the status quo.",
